{
  "version": 5,
  "name": "How-Kirchhoffs-Circuit-Laws-Work-Two-Source-v3",
  "description": "Three branches meet at one junction. The left branch is a 9 volt source (E1) in series with a 1 kilohm resistor (R1). The middle branch is a 3 kilohm resistor (R2) down to ground. The right branch is a 5 volt source (E2) in series with a 1 kilohm resistor (R3). Working it out with Kirchhoff's two laws puts the junction at 6 volts. That makes R1 carry 3 milliamps toward the junction, R2 carry 2 milliamps to ground, and R3 carry 1 milliamp the wrong way, back into the 5 volt source, which is therefore being charged. The current entering the junction (3 mA) equals the current leaving it (2 mA plus 1 mA).",
  "wheelLocked": false,
  "activeTab": "t1",
  "nextIndex": 2,
